title: The Golden Twenties
subtitle: Economic Prosperity and Republican Optimism
is-arrival-scene: false

-- on-arrival

It is April 1928. The Weimar Republic enters a period of remarkable economic prosperity. The hyperinflation crisis of 1923 is a distant memory. The Dawes Plan has stabilized the currency. Foreign investment flows into Germany—primarily American capital seeking returns in the recovering European economy.

The streets of Berlin, Frankfurt, and Munich buzz with energy. New buildings rise. Theaters overflow with audiences. Jazz clubs proliferate. The arts flourish in a remarkable cultural renaissance.

This is the Golden Twenties.

[Continue]

(set: $golden-twenties-active: true)

---

title: Golden Twenties - Main Decision
id: golden-twenties-main
on-arrival:

The economic boom creates a powerful psychological effect. Citizens who feared the republic might be beginning to trust it.

"Perhaps Weimar can work after all," people say in the cafés and beer halls.

You witness this shift firsthand. The radical parties—both communist and fascist—find their rhetoric falling on deafer ears. People are too busy building, creating, investing in the future.

Your party faces a choice: How will you respond to this golden moment?

[Support republican consolidation]
(set: $golden-twenties-choice: "consolidate")
(go-to: golden-twenties-consolidate)

[Focus on economic gains for your supporters]
(set: $golden-twenties-choice: "party-benefit")
(go-to: golden-twenties-party-focused)

[Remain cautious about this fragile prosperity]
(set: $golden-twenties-choice: "cautious")
(go-to: golden-twenties-skeptical)

---

title: Consolidating the Republic
id: golden-twenties-consolidate
on-arrival:

(set: $republic-support-increase: 2)

Your party throws its full support behind strengthening republican institutions.

You advocate for strengthening democratic safeguards, supporting republican youth organizations, and funding educational programs promoting democracy.

The message resonates. In prosperity, citizens are more receptive to arguments about stability.

"The republic has delivered prosperity. Now let us secure it for the future," becomes your party's theme.

Support for republican values increases noticeably. Moderate voices gain influence. The radical parties find fewer recruits.

(set: $republic-support: ($republic-support or 0) + $republic-support-increase)

[End scenario]
(go-to: golden-twenties-end)

---

title: Party-Focused Approach
id: golden-twenties-party-focused
on-arrival:

(set: $republic-support-increase: 0)

Your party prioritizes using the economic boom to benefit its own members and supporters.

You secure lucrative government contracts, create party-controlled job programs, build party-aligned organizations.

This strategy works—your party grows richer. But you neglect the broader republican project.

Other parties follow suit. The golden age becomes less about strengthening democracy and more about factions competing for advantage.

The republic stabilizes through economic inertia, not genuine democratic commitment. Republican values don't strengthen.

(set: $republic-support: ($republic-support or 0) + $republic-support-increase)

[End scenario]
(go-to: golden-twenties-end)

---

title: The Cautious Response
id: golden-twenties-skeptical
on-arrival:

(set: $republic-support-increase: 0.5)

Your party warns that the golden prosperity rests on unstable foundations.

You argue the economy depends too heavily on foreign capital, which could be withdrawn at any moment. The underlying industrial structure remains fragile.

While your warnings contain truth, the public finds them grating during prosperity. Your party's cautious stance is seen as negative.

Other parties criticize you for undermining confidence. Yet you have preserved credibility for the crisis ahead.

(set: $republic-support: ($republic-support or 0) + $republic-support-increase)

[End scenario]
(go-to: golden-twenties-end)

---

title: The Golden Twenties - End
id: golden-twenties-end
on-arrival:

(set: $golden-twenties-scenario-complete: true)

The spring and summer of 1928 unfold under clear skies.

Berlin becomes the cultural capital of Europe. The Bauhaus redesigns the built environment. Intellectuals debate grand visions of the future.

The October 1928 elections approach with the republic seemingly vindicated. Prosperity has been achieved. Democracy appears to be working.

Yet in the shadows—unnoticed by most—new forces gather:

- In Munich, a failed painter watches Germany's youth
- In Washington, stockbrokers make ever-riskier investments
- In the universities, ideological movements stir
- In Moscow, decisions are made that will trigger global consequences

The golden age will last perhaps another year. Then the storm will come.

But for now, the republic has gained something precious: time, and proof that democracy can deliver prosperity.

Republic Support Change: +$republic-support-increase %

